WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:09.040
Okay, so what is exactly ordered by you can use this type of clause in order to sort your data.

00:09.040 --> 00:12.720
And of course, in order to sort your data, you have to decide on two mechanisms.

00:12.760 --> 00:18.400
Either you want to sort your data ascending from the lowest value to the highest value, or exactly

00:18.400 --> 00:22.400
the opposite way, using descending from the highest value to the lowest.

00:22.400 --> 00:24.200
And the syntax can looks like this.

00:24.200 --> 00:30.040
So as usual, we start with the select, then from and after the from, you can specify order by and.

00:30.040 --> 00:34.440
With that you are telling SQL we have to sort the data and you have to specify two things.

00:34.480 --> 00:40.000
First you have to specify for SQL the column that should be used in order to sort the results.

00:40.000 --> 00:41.840
So for example you can say score.

00:41.840 --> 00:44.760
And after the column name you have to specify the mechanism.

00:44.760 --> 00:48.000
So for example you say ascending from the lowest to the highest.

00:48.000 --> 00:53.120
And in SQL if you don't specify the mechanism the default is going to be ascending.

00:53.120 --> 00:57.040
So you will not get an error if you don't specify anything after the column name.

00:57.040 --> 01:03.730
But my advice here is always to specify Something after the column easier because it's just straightforward

01:03.730 --> 01:05.050
and easier to understand.

01:05.050 --> 01:08.610
And if someone reads it, can understand immediately it's going to be ascending.

01:08.610 --> 01:12.170
Because maybe not everyone knows what is the default in SQL.

01:12.170 --> 01:15.930
So always specify a value, even if it's like easier to skip it.

01:15.930 --> 01:20.650
And if you want to store the data from the highest to the lowest, then you can specify descending.

01:20.650 --> 01:23.010
So as usual SQL can go and start from the from.

01:23.010 --> 01:25.210
It's going to go and grab your data from database.

01:25.210 --> 01:28.770
Then the second step is going to go and sort the results.

01:28.770 --> 01:30.610
So the order by is going to be executed.

01:30.610 --> 01:33.370
And SQL is going to say okay, I'm going to sort it by the score.

01:33.370 --> 01:39.330
And using descending mechanism and SQL can go and start like moving around your rows where the first

01:39.330 --> 01:42.130
row is going to be the customer with the highest score.

01:42.130 --> 01:45.770
And in this example John has the highest score, the 900.

01:45.770 --> 01:49.170
So John can appear as a first row at the results.

01:49.170 --> 01:50.650
And that's because his score.

01:50.650 --> 01:54.370
And after that the second highest is going to be George with 750.

01:54.410 --> 01:56.850
And SQL is going to go and keep sorting the data.

01:56.850 --> 02:03.180
And then we have 500 then Than 350, and the last row going to be the customer with the lowest score.

02:03.340 --> 02:03.900
The zero.

02:03.900 --> 02:06.580
So this is how SQL executes your order by now.

02:06.580 --> 02:08.540
Let's go back to SQL in order to practice.

02:08.540 --> 02:08.900
All right.

02:08.900 --> 02:10.180
So now we have the following task.

02:10.180 --> 02:15.980
And it says retrieve all customers and sort the result by the highest score first.

02:15.980 --> 02:18.620
So now by looking to the task we need all the customers.

02:18.620 --> 02:21.540
So there is like no conditions or anything to filter.

02:21.540 --> 02:23.260
But we have to sort the results.

02:23.260 --> 02:24.500
So let's go and do that.

02:24.500 --> 02:29.340
So we're going to start as usual by selecting all the columns from the table customers.

02:29.340 --> 02:32.460
So now if you go and execute it you will get all your customers.

02:32.460 --> 02:35.780
And you are now seeing the data exactly like stored in the database.

02:35.780 --> 02:39.060
And you can see the result is not sorted by the scores.

02:39.060 --> 02:42.900
So we have here a low score, then high score, then low and so on.

02:42.900 --> 02:45.500
Now the task says we have to sort the results.

02:45.500 --> 02:48.140
So we have to go and use the order by.

02:48.180 --> 02:50.180
And now you have to understand from which column.

02:50.180 --> 02:51.940
And we can get that from the task.

02:51.940 --> 02:54.700
So it says it should be sorted by the score.

02:54.700 --> 02:56.700
So we're going to go and define the score here.

02:56.700 --> 03:01.470
And the final thing that you have to define is the mechanism descending or ascending, and you can get

03:01.470 --> 03:02.510
it as well from the task.

03:02.510 --> 03:05.470
So we have to sort the data by the highest score first.

03:05.470 --> 03:07.550
So the highest first and then the lowest.

03:07.550 --> 03:10.350
So that means we're going to go and use that descending.

03:10.350 --> 03:11.350
So that's all.

03:11.390 --> 03:12.710
Let's go and execute it.

03:12.750 --> 03:16.150
Now as you can see in the results the first customer has the highest score.

03:16.230 --> 03:21.230
Then we have the second one with the second highest until the last one with the lowest score.

03:21.270 --> 03:21.790
That's it.

03:21.790 --> 03:23.070
This is how you sort your data.

03:23.070 --> 03:24.710
And with that we have solved the task.

03:24.710 --> 03:26.750
Now let's do exactly the opposite.

03:26.750 --> 03:30.270
So we want to sort the results by the lowest score first.

03:30.270 --> 03:33.630
So that means we want to see first the customers with the lowest score.

03:33.670 --> 03:38.910
Like here in this example we should see the ID number five as the first because he has the lowest score

03:38.950 --> 03:39.470
the zero.

03:39.510 --> 03:42.870
Now in order to do that all what you have to do is to switch the mechanism.

03:42.870 --> 03:45.270
Instead of descending, we can use ascending.

03:45.310 --> 03:46.670
Let's go and execute it.

03:46.670 --> 03:47.310
And that's it.

03:47.310 --> 03:49.550
As you can see now we have the lowest score.

03:49.590 --> 03:54.950
Then the second lowest score until the last row it's going to be the customer with the highest score.

03:54.950 --> 03:57.150
So the lowest score comes first.

03:57.190 --> 03:58.280
So it is very simple.

03:58.280 --> 04:00.400
This is how you sort your data using SQL.

04:04.280 --> 04:07.160
And now I'm going to show you one more thing that you can do with the order by.

04:07.200 --> 04:11.800
You can sort your data using multiple columns and we call it nested sorting.

04:11.840 --> 04:16.640
So now let's take this very simple example where you want to sort your data using country.

04:16.640 --> 04:19.360
So we are saying order by the column country.

04:19.360 --> 04:21.240
And the mechanism is going to be ascending.

04:21.240 --> 04:23.000
So from the lowest to the highest.

04:23.040 --> 04:27.120
Now if you do that SQL can go and sort the data this time based on the country.

04:27.120 --> 04:31.480
So we're going to have like the first two customers from Germany is sorting it alphabetically.

04:31.480 --> 04:34.640
Then we have the UK and the last two are going to be from USA.

04:34.680 --> 04:37.920
Now if you are checking the final results, you might say, you know what?

04:37.920 --> 04:39.280
There is like something wrong.

04:39.280 --> 04:42.040
The data is not completely sorted correctly.

04:42.040 --> 04:47.040
So if you are looking to the first two customers that come from country Germany, you can see the scores

04:47.080 --> 04:50.840
are sorted in ascending way from the lowest to the highest.

04:50.840 --> 04:54.040
So first we have 350, then 500, then UK.

04:54.080 --> 04:56.040
It's fine because we have only one customer.

04:56.080 --> 04:58.260
Now if you look to the customers from USA.

04:58.300 --> 05:00.860
You see that it is like sorted the way around.

05:00.860 --> 05:03.940
It is sorted, descending from the highest to the lowest.

05:03.940 --> 05:06.420
So first we have the score 900, then zero.

05:06.420 --> 05:11.300
So there is like no clean way on how the data is sorted and the result is not really clean.

05:11.300 --> 05:16.580
And this issue happens usually if you are sorting your data based in a column that has repetition,

05:16.620 --> 05:19.460
like here, the country we have twice Germany and twice USA.

05:19.500 --> 05:25.860
So now in order to refine the sorting and make it more correct, we can include in the sorting another

05:25.860 --> 05:26.380
column.

05:26.380 --> 05:28.260
And this scenario for example the score.

05:28.260 --> 05:33.180
So we can make a list of columns in the order by and we can separate them using the comma.

05:33.180 --> 05:35.820
And of course you can have different mechanism for each column.

05:35.820 --> 05:38.180
Like for the country we are seeing it is ascending.

05:38.180 --> 05:41.140
But for the score we say, you know what, let's make a descending.

05:41.140 --> 05:43.780
It will not be only one for all columns.

05:43.780 --> 05:47.580
So now what can happen is we can start sorting the data for each section.

05:47.580 --> 05:52.140
So for the two customers from Germany, the sorting going to be from the highest to the lowest.

05:52.140 --> 05:54.540
So it can go and switch the two customers.

05:54.540 --> 05:58.270
So Martin going to be first because he has higher score than Mario.

05:58.270 --> 06:01.350
And with that, we are refining the scores based on the same value.

06:01.390 --> 06:05.710
Of course, the country now for the UK, nothing going to happen because we have only one value.

06:05.710 --> 06:11.470
And for the USA as well, nothing can happen because it is already sorted in the correct way from the

06:11.470 --> 06:12.390
highest to the lowest.

06:12.390 --> 06:17.150
So as you can see, if you are including a second column, you are refining your sorting.

06:17.150 --> 06:20.110
And as well my friends, the order is very important.

06:20.110 --> 06:23.470
So this is how you can do nested sorting in SQL.

06:23.510 --> 06:26.030
Let's go back to our SQL and start practicing.

06:26.070 --> 06:26.390
All right.

06:26.390 --> 06:27.550
So now we have the following task.

06:27.550 --> 06:34.830
And it says retrieve all customers and sort the results by the country and then by the highest score.

06:34.870 --> 06:36.710
So again we need all customers.

06:36.710 --> 06:39.670
So select everything from customers table.

06:39.710 --> 06:43.030
And now the task says we have to sort the result by the country.

06:43.030 --> 06:44.990
So we're going to start with the order by.

06:44.990 --> 06:49.630
And since it says by the country we're going to go with the country and we're going to sort it alphabetically.

06:49.630 --> 06:51.070
So it's going to be ascending.

06:51.070 --> 06:52.350
So let's go execute it.

06:52.350 --> 06:56.030
Now you can see the data is sorted completely differently by the country.

06:56.120 --> 06:58.680
So we have first Germany, UK and then USA.

06:58.720 --> 06:59.960
But that's not all.

07:00.000 --> 07:02.880
And says then by the highest score.

07:02.920 --> 07:05.880
So we have to go and include another column in the sorting.

07:05.880 --> 07:10.360
And we can go and add that by adding a comma and then mention another column the score.

07:10.360 --> 07:12.320
And now we have to specify the mechanism.

07:12.360 --> 07:14.280
It says by the highest score.

07:14.280 --> 07:16.000
So the highest must come first.

07:16.000 --> 07:17.760
And with that we are using descending.

07:17.800 --> 07:22.920
Now what is the current situation is that if you look to the results, for example, for those two customers

07:22.920 --> 07:25.360
we have 350 and then 500.

07:25.360 --> 07:28.480
So that means the scores are sorted ascending right.

07:28.520 --> 07:29.920
The same thing for USA.

07:30.120 --> 07:31.760
So from the lowest to the highest.

07:31.800 --> 07:36.080
Now if you go and do it like this, what can happen is you can go and switch it.

07:36.080 --> 07:37.240
So you can see over here.

07:37.280 --> 07:42.080
Now for Germany, first comes the highest the 500 and then the 350.

07:42.120 --> 07:44.120
And for USA as well they switched.

07:44.120 --> 07:46.080
So we have the highest and then the lowest.

07:46.080 --> 07:47.520
And with that we have solved the task.

07:47.520 --> 07:50.160
Now again the order of those columns are very important.

07:50.160 --> 07:56.090
So since the scores comes after the country we will not get the highest scores first at are the results.

07:56.090 --> 07:59.010
So we will not get the 900 as a first row.

07:59.050 --> 08:02.650
And that's because the scores must be sorted after the country.

08:02.650 --> 08:04.490
So the country has more priority.

08:04.530 --> 08:06.050
Now if you go and flip that.

08:06.050 --> 08:10.410
So let's go over here and say sort first the score and then the country.

08:10.410 --> 08:11.810
So let's go and execute it.

08:11.890 --> 08:16.650
School has first to sort the scores so that you will get the 901st.

08:16.650 --> 08:17.050
Right.

08:17.050 --> 08:18.410
And then the countries.

08:18.450 --> 08:22.690
And since there is like no duplicates in the scores, this makes no sense at all.

08:22.690 --> 08:24.090
So you can go and skip it.

08:24.090 --> 08:28.490
So nested sorting only makes sense if you have repetition in your results.

08:29.050 --> 08:34.170
And you can use the help of a second column in order to make the sorting perfect.

08:34.170 --> 08:34.930
So that's it.

08:34.930 --> 08:37.050
And with that of course we have solved the task.

08:37.050 --> 08:37.450
All right.

08:37.450 --> 08:41.530
So with that you have learned how to sort your data using order by now.

08:41.530 --> 08:47.130
In the next tip we're going to talk about how to aggregate and group up your data using groupby.

08:47.130 --> 08:51.890
And we're going to put it between the where and the order by because in the order of the query, the

08:51.890 --> 08:54.770
groupby comes between the where and the order by.

08:54.810 --> 08:55.530
So let's go.
